WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.020
I’m getting hit with some major whiplash
going from Velma to The Last of Us because

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.940
it’s from the absolute bottom of the barrel
to the peak of entertainment.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.940
The Last of Us Episode 1 blew away my expectations
because it elevates the source material which

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.170
was already outstanding on its own.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.680
In this day and age great adaptions are rare
because it requires the writers of the show

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.680
to have a thorough understanding of what made
the source material great and how to properly

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.700
translate it to the new medium.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.240
Craig Mazin, who is the showrunner, is doing
an amazing job so far and The Last of Us may

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.870
have the potential to rival Chernobyl which
was his last project, and that is an insane

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.950
statement on its own from how perfect that
miniseries was.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.300
Also since they have Neil Druckman helping
with the scripts, it really goes to show that

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.030
they want to be as respectful to the source
material as possible and to be authentic.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.920
For the show, it’s able to build off of
the rich world of the game and add in scenes

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.850
that further flesh out the world.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.820
Along with having more scenes dedicated to
character development and altering perspectives

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.060
that you can’t really do in a game because
you are tied to a singular point of view.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.070
As an example, we have more scenes with Ellie
to build her up a little more and to allow

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.930
us to sympathize with her quicker.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.640
On top of great world building like the meeting
with the fireflies and them briefly going

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.280
over their objectives.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.080
This scene was technically exposition driven
but it didn’t feel boring at all because

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.180
the writers are putting a lot of emphasis
on the characters.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.430
These people, despite being in only a couple
of small scenes, have more personality than

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.500
most other main characters from other shows.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.290
These additional scenes that aren’t in the
game really standout to me and is what impressed

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.670
me because of how much.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.610
Like the first scene alone was actually one
of my favorite scenes from the episode.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:41.010
Besides obviously the tragic fate of Sarah
which was just as good as it was in the game,

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.170
but I want to first appreciate the new content
within the show.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.540
This cold open is a masterclass of a scene
within itself.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:52.030
Something that Craig Mazin does really well
is instill a sense of dread within the audience.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.000
There’s a lot of different factors that
goes into this like the cinematography getting

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.030
tighter and more claustrophobic by pushing
in on close-ups as the scene progresses, shots

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:06.070
focusing on the audience to reveal their scared
reactions to us, and the music slowly building

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:13.150
and permeating in the background to make you
feel uneasy.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.959
Along with the monologue that the scientist
delivers being expertly written.

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:21.290
A small detail I loved was how the scene first
starts out with a bit of comedy, but as the

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.819
doctor goes through his speech he even rattles
the interviewer which is really rare and makes

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:27.620
this occasion even more unsettling.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.101
I also just found the whole fungi revelation
really intriguing and is honestly one of the

00:02:32.101 --> 00:02:34.930
most believable reasons for a zombie outbreak.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.040
In the game, I don’t remember them explicitly
explaining the reason for the outbreak and

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.840
you know it has to do with fungus.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.330
But for the show, it further fleshes out this
aspect within the world and adds to the source

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.330
material.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:50.590
So right away Craig Mazin creates a tense
atmosphere and writes an original scene with

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.080
so much weight and expertise that it instantly
engaged me and made me excited for what’s

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.080
to come.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.590
Because this show offers more than just a
carbon copy of the original game.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.360
The cold open was also followed by a great
opening title sequence.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.080
In particular I love how it looked like the
fungus was spreading through the America.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.920
And I’m really glad that they are using
the original score from the game.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:10.920
Checking IMDB, it’s nice to see that they
are bringing back the original composer to

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:11.920
work on the show.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.909
Another original scene or scenes that I loved
was Sarah’s relationship with her neighbors.

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:20.269
This again elevates and builds upon the game
since the opening of the game quickly throws

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.680
you into the outbreak and the car sequence.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.260
For the show, they were able to build more
suspense through this development and one

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.250
of my favorite shots from the first episode
that achieved this was when Sarah was looking

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.879
at the DVD’s and the old lady behind her
started having facial contortions.

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:39.290
Just an excellent shot that is very creative
through its usage of hiding a subject with

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.290
focus.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.260
It gives of this feeling of discovery within
the frame and further scares you as a viewer

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.769
when you notice what is happening in the background.

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:50.709
Along with not really getting a detailed view
as to what is happening so it further gets

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:51.709
under your skin.

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:55.930
And the shot after this where Sarah’s positioning
within the frame blocking the door behind

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:59.650
her creates the feeling that she may get approached
from behind.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.400
Just through these two shots alone, I’m
already in love with the cinematography.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.880
Which the cinematography overall for the first
episode was beautiful.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.890
The design of the show is also just immaculate
and allows the cinematographer to get some

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:11.890
incredible footage.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.310
The production design is filled to the brim
with detail and there is so much storytelling

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.850
being done with just the environment alone.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:23.070
This makes me really excited for the next
episode to see how the set-pieces look when

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.850
they are in the run down city.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.479
This atmosphere with the guitar centric score
playing in the background is so god damn good

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:32.620
and gives me great memories from my playthrough
of the game.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.040
Since I was already gushing about how good
the writing is, I want to further acknowledge

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.979
how intelligent the script is.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:41.560
There’s so many moments that don’t hold
your hand in terms of what’s happening and

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.330
allows the viewer to fully engage with the
show.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.650
Moments like the child going through the Boston
checkpoint and us figuring out that he or

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.220
she is infected.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:53.820
At this point we don’t know exactly how
the tests work, despite it being red, so ironically

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.870
this girl officer confirms through overexaggerated
kindness what the fate of the kid is.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.470
Also to point out a brilliant edit, the end
of the scene hard cuts to the bonfire of bodies,

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.580
which to me implied that the kid died, but
they did bring him or her out so that we could

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.509
punctuate how far gone and detached joel currently
is.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:14.690
Another clever moment in the writing was the
ending with the radio.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.100
Through a bit of character development in
showing that Ellie is sharp and observant,

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.180
she tries to fill in the missing piece for
the radio code and figures out that 80’s

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.630
music means trouble.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:27.449
So since we retained that bit of information
it turns on again playing a song from the

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:31.030
80’s which then builds and climaxes at the
end of the episode.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.100
This rewards the viewer for paying attention
and gives them that moment of “ahhh I get

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.250
it”.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.669
Even the dialogue for the characters feels
very natural and likeable.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:43.819
An example I liked was Joel and Sarah having
breakfast and joking about how there are egg

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:45.030
shells in the eggs.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:49.950
It becomes a quick re-curring joke for them
in this scene and builds their close relationship.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.160
In the game, it starts with Sarah giving Joel
his birthday present.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.419
So the show is able to further build the relationship
between them and that thus makes their ending

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:00.360
even more devastating because they have a
stronger bond.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.160
It’s kind of surprising really how much
new content there is within this first half

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.729
hour because it all perfectly fits into the
source material and feels like a natural extension

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:10.140
that aids it.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:14.750
Like looking at the game, that prologue only
lasts about 15 minutes and in the show we

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.569
don’t get to the time jump until the 35
minute mark.

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:20.610
Then for the escape in the truck it was perfectly
done.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:25.000
The sense of dread by this point was built
up so much to the point that even if you played

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.310
the game you probably felt some form of anxiety.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.780
Me and my girlfriend played through The Last
of Us together and even she was getting nervous

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.440
through this scene despite knowing their outcome.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.630
Also the execution of this scene was as close
as you could possibly get to the game and

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:42.220
if anything I think they actually improved
it with the plane crash.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.290
Those planes looming over the characters and
deafening them further increased the tension

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.560
and made the scene even more frightening.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.880
So when one finally crashed it was a literal
great explosive climax.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.510
For the game, they end up just getting hit
by a car which then flips their vehicle.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:59.082
So for the show, I think this was definitely
a step up to a degree.

00:06:59.082 --> 00:07:00.082
Also in the game, this entire sequence is
done without cutting the camera, so it was

00:07:00.082 --> 00:07:01.082
nice that they kind of matched this within
the show by utilizing long takes.

00:07:01.082 --> 00:07:04.699
When Joel eventually runs into the infected
it was absolutely metal how crazy they were

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:06.720
and how they were running full tilt into things.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.169
It makes the stunts for that scene very impressive
and it reminded me of World War Z.

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:15.690
Which this then leads into the death of Sarah
and it was as emotionally pungent as it was

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.690
in the game.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.030
Pedro Pascal is a perfect Joel and his heartbreaking
moment with Sarah left me floored and on the

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.879
verge of tears even though I’ve already
played through this moment.

00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:27.830
Then Bella Ramsey so far is doing a stellar
job as Ellie.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.070
Her opening scene quickly establishes her
character and was a funny bit of writing.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:36.350
Her and Joel are an integral part of what
makes the Last of Us so amazing and so far

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.690
from the brief moments they’ve shared together
it’s pretty promising.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:45.780
If anything there’s one small change they
made in which the circumstances Joel and Ellie

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.970
meet are a bit more negative like how Joel
constantly has a gun trained on Ellie.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:54.099
While in the game she does come at them with
the knife, but she opens up a bit more to

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:55.099
them.

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:58.360
So in the show I like how there’s a bit
more starting friction and I’m excited to

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.910
see how their relationship blossoms.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:05.310
So overall the first episode of The Last of
Us essentially blows any other video game

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:09.669
adaption out of the water so much to the point
that there’s basically no competition.

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:13.410
I’m just really happy they didn’t try
to turn this into a movie and are actually

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.280
dedicating an entire series to telling the
story.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:20.139
Thank you for watching and right now I’m
leaning towards covering this show weekly.

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:23.980
Given that it’s actually really good and
probably has some carry over from the game

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.860
of thrones crowd since both shows are on HBO
Max.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.479
If I do cover the show weekly, I’ll also
try to fit in some other videos so I’m not

00:08:30.479 --> 00:08:32.779
just doing the Last of Us for the next two
months.

